---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/conduit-fill';
import { CONDUITS, CONDUIT_SIZES, maxWires, gaugeLabel, type Gauge } from '../lib/electrical';

const gauges: Gauge[] = ['14', '12', '10', '8', '6', '4'];
const sizes = CONDUIT_SIZES.slice(0, 6);
---

<CalcPage
  slug="conduit-fill-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Conduit Fill Calculator: EMT & PVC, NEC Chapter 9"
  description="Free conduit fill calculator. Enter conduit type and size and the THHN wires inside to get the fill percentage against NEC limits, plus the maximum wires per conduit size."
  h1="Conduit Fill Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Check whether your wires fit: fill percentage for EMT and PVC conduit against the NEC Chapter 9 limits, the maximum
    number of THHN wires, and the smallest conduit that works.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="limits">NEC conduit fill limits</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Number of conductors</th><th class="num">Maximum fill</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td class="num">53%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td class="num">31%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 or more</td><td class="num">40%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Fill is the total cross-sectional area of the wires, including insulation, divided by the inside area of the conduit.
    Equipment grounding conductors count toward the fill.
  </p>

  <h2 id="example">Example: four 12 AWG THHN in ¾ in EMT</h2>
  <p>
    Each 12 AWG THHN wire is 0.0133 in², so four total 0.0532 in². ¾ in EMT has 0.533 in² inside, making the fill 10%,
    well under the 40% limit. The same conduit holds up to 16 of these wires.
  </p>

  <h2 id="emt-table">Maximum THHN wires in EMT</h2>
  <p>Same-size conductors, using the same method as NEC Annex C, Table C.1.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Wire</th>{sizes.map((s) => <th class="num">{s} in</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{gauges.map((g) => <tr><td>{gaugeLabel(g)}</td>{sizes.map((s) => <td class="num">{maxWires('emt', s, g)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pvc-table">Maximum THHN wires in PVC Schedule 40</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Wire</th>{sizes.map((s) => <th class="num">{s} in</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{gauges.map((g) => <tr><td>{gaugeLabel(g)}</td>{sizes.map((s) => <td class="num">{maxWires('pvc40', s, g)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Long run? <a href="/voltage-drop-calculator/">Check the voltage drop</a> for the wire size you picked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Does a full conduit mean the wires are properly sized?</h3>
  <p>
    No. With more than three current-carrying conductors in one conduit, NEC 310.15(C)(1) requires reducing each wire's
    ampacity, for example to 80% for four to six conductors. Check sizing with the
    <a href="/wire-size-calculator/">wire size calculator</a> and apply the adjustment.
  </p>
  <h3>Why is PVC smaller inside than EMT of the same size?</h3>
  <p>
    Trade size is nominal. PVC walls are thicker, and Schedule 80 thicker still, so they hold fewer wires. Use the
    conduit you are actually installing.
  </p>
  <h3>What about other wire types?</h3>
  <p>
    This calculator uses THHN/THWN-2 dimensions, the most common building wire in conduit. XHHW, bare grounds, and
    others have different areas listed in NEC Chapter 9 Tables 5 and 8.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Conduit areas from NEC Chapter 9 Table 4 (EMT, PVC Schedule 40 and 80); wire areas from Table 5 (THHN/THWN-2).</li>
    <li>Allowed area is the conduit area at 53%, 31%, or 40% per Table 1, rounded to three decimals as published.</li>
    <li>For planning only; your inspector and local code have the final word.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
